[Music]  so  [Music]  coming up on your weekly news show a  kite festival to remind us about the  plight of refugees from afghanistan the  people used to fly them a lot before  they got banned by the taliban  and clearing up our oceans a teenager on  a mission to get rid of marine litter  the oceans around us in the uk are  really important and if you go diving  down there it's amazing  [Music]  hello if you want to know what's going  on in the world right now you've come to  the right place yep and this week we're  looking at the fallout from two  different conflicts starting with the  war in ukraine yeah it's been six months  since russia invaded ukraine and the  fighting goes on millions of families  have had to flee their country and you  may have even had some ukrainian kids  joining your school but why is russia  doing this and what's it all about if  you don't get it don't worry because  i've been trying to find out  to truly understand why ukraine has been  invaded by russia we need to hit the  history books and go back inside  [Music]  from 1917 when the royal russian family  were overthrown  until 1991  we saw the rise and the fall of the  soviet union or the ussr ukraine was one  of the 15 states that made up this giant  country  all of them became independent countries  when the soviet union's political system  and economy collapsed in 1991 and its  people demanded change russia was the  country with the biggest power  surrounded by smaller states as you can  see here  russia's current president vladimir  putin came to power in 2000 he has  always made it clear that the end of the  soviet union angers him and some world  leaders believe he would like russia to  become a huge empire similar to how the  soviet union used to be back in the day  and he wants to claim back some of the  former soviet states  in 2008 russia invaded neighbouring  georgia and took control of a large part  of the country  it's very much like what he's doing with  ukraine now  trying to take back a former state that  largely wants to be independent of  russian control  2014 is a good year to look at the  relationship between ukraine and russia  the then president of ukraine with a  close relationship to russia faced huge  protests demanding he quit and when he  did russia's vladimir putin thought that  ukraine was about to become more  friendly with western countries like the  uk  and the usa  so he sent in russian soldiers to take  control of crimea which is an area in  the south of ukraine  let's travel in time right up to the  present day the ukraine invasion is now  a very complicated situation  rebels are people who go against the  government in power  in february russia sent in its troops to  support rebels from the regions of  luhansk and donetsk the rebels wanted to  be independent from ukraine  so rebels with russians began fighting  against the ukrainian army  but the most important thing to know is  that this was just the start of a  full-scale invasion of ukraine  president putin appeared on russian tv  to want other countries not to try and  stop them  there is huge support for ukraine here  in the west for all his bombs and tanks  and missiles i don't believe that the  russian dictator will ever subdue the  national feeling of the ukrainians and  their passionate belief that their  country should be free  there are still large russian  communities in many former soviet states  including ukraine while some would like  to be part of russia world leaders say  president putin has no right to wade in  six months on from the start of the  invasion many cities lie in ruins  and the russians have taken control of  large areas in the south and the east of  the country  but ukrainian forces are refusing to  give in and with help of weapons supply  from western countries including the uk  president zelinski remains determined to  defend his country  [Music]  for ukrainian children living in the uk  hearing about the war can be very  upsetting for them as they still have  friends and family back home so we're  being joined by dr chris kandaya he's  part of a charity called the sanctuary  foundation they've been helping loads of  ukrainian people fleeing the war to find  host families here in the uk so am i  right in saying that you've got someone  staying with you that's right we've got  a lovely lady called natasha she feels  already part of our family even though  we've only known her a month she's a  fantastic cook and so we've tried this  new food to us called borscht which is  actually a cabbage soup which doesn't  sound very appetizing but it's really  good so for many ukrainian children who  have joined our school they still have  friends and family back home how should  we be helping them through this  difficult time that's such a good  question maya lots of the kids that are  here are just with their mums and  they're worried about their uncles and  their fathers and their grandparents i  think for kids arriving at a school  often you don't want to stick out so you  don't want to be known as the refugee or  the ukrainian just just another kid and  so i think just being sensitive asking  people if they're okay you know  including them in things making sure  that they feel part of the gang rather  than outside i think everybody can do  that as well they're not you know  everybody can welcome someone in a  friend group exactly i think sometimes  little things like trying to learn a  little bit of the ukrainian language  might be a nice way that you're showing  value to another person they're so happy  when you just say a little thing like uh  yaks bravo means how are you yes bravo  yeah oh mate you're a natural right  someone asked you that you say  does your daughter which means  great so just little things like this  just help people know that we're there  for them we're listening and we're doing  what we can to make them feel welcome a  lot of families are facing uncertainty  about where they'll live once their  agreement with the uk families come to  an end and that's a lot of pressure for  them really isn't it it is it would be  great if we could help people stay where  they are and we've got a little campaign  that we're running at sanctuary  foundation getting both our ukrainian  friends and those that are hosting them  to write a letter to the government so  you could encourage people to make use  of that and i think something not a lot  of people like think about is you know  what about the russian children can't  even imagine what would be going through  their head when they see what their own  army is doing yeah it's so difficult i  think for a lot of russians in russia  and russians that are here they're not  supporting what's going on that's not  their army that's their government's  army and they're not behind it so i  think that's another thing that we can  do try to bring peace and reconciliation  in our schools and not allow prejudice  and stereotypes to rule thank you so  much for talking to us oh it's my  pleasure  now there's nothing we love more at fyi  than trying to spot fake news so let's  see if you can figure out which one of  these stories is dodgy  [Music]  the world's richest man elon musk has  told millions of people on social media  that he's buying manchester united but  could that really be true  or how about this story apparently sean  the sheep is going on nasa's latest moon  mission does that sound like fake news  or is it one giant leap for lamkin  [Music]  still to come on this week's programme  underwater litter picking to try and  save our planet  [Music]  it's been just over a year since british  and american forces pulled out of  afghanistan leaving a militant group  called the taliban to take control  many afghan people were worried about  this because the taliban have very  strict rules about the way people can  live their lives yeah the history of  this is quite complicated isn't it back  in 2001 america and its allies including  the uk  invaded afghanistan because the taliban  was supporting terrorist groups  but as soon as british and american  troops left the country last year the  taliban came straight back into power  they follow an extreme version of islam  and they don't allow girls to get an  education with the taliban back in  control afghan people who'd worked with  the british military were in great  danger so the uk agreed to take in more  than 20 000 refugees  but a year on many of them are still  living in temporary hotels yeah so to  mark the one year anniversary some  people who fled to the uk have been  flying kites and i went along to find  out why  have been one of the oldest traditions  of afghanistan it's 800 years old if not  more  the people used to fly them a lot before  they got banned by the taliban  it's more than a pastime it's an art  form it's something that brings  everybody together  arrows is 15 years old and was born in  the uk but still has fun memories of  flying kites with his family who are  back home in afghanistan they've been  living under the taliban rule for just  over a year now kites whenever we lift  our kites high into the sky it just  reminds me of a thunderstorm we used to  go there every day with our cousins and  family and friends we used to have a  fight with kites  this kite flying festival is a chance  for everyone here to celebrate their  culture and to remind people of the  plight of afghan refugees  there was a time when the taliban banned  kite flying as well as many other  expressions of joy like music and  dancing so keeping the tradition alive  is hugely important for african refugees  who are trying to make a new home in the  uk so i'm here with jay and jaden can  you show me how to fly the kites sure  you just tuck it up and then i've seen  people holding it and then  voila  in afghanistan they tried obviously  evacuating from there but it was  unpossible because the taliban had  flocked everywhere they blocked the  airports the borders to each country i  just want them to just carry on with  what's going on until we're finished and  get sorted out  [Music]  i've met so many different people and  i've learned so much about the afghani  culture but what's really opened my eyes  is that no matter where some of these  people are in the world they still make  time for their culture even if it's  something as simple as coming out on a  warm windy day to come fly a kite and  it's really lovely how many people came  to support them  it's so important to hold on to to the  roots and to what makes afghanistan  my country my land it's the music it's  the food it's the kites in the sky it's  the pigeons over kabul  [Music]  check out this summer holiday video  that's gone viral on social media these  guys are on a beach on the greek island  of scathos but it also happens to be  right next to the airport runway hold on  to your sun hats  [Music]  now this is quite shocking  water companies have been pumping  untreated sewage water into the sea at  over 50 beaches in the uk  it's because some drains and sewers  couldn't handle the amount of heavy rain  some parts of the uk have had just  recently yeah things got so bad that  swimmers were told it wasn't safe to go  in the water but it's not just sewage  you need to look out for the equivalent  of a truckload of plastic waste is  dumped into our oceans every single  minute it's bad for marine life but it  also contributes to climate change  oliver who lives near bristol wanted to  show us how he's doing his bit to try  and make a difference  [Music]  today we're in wales we're in  pembrokeshire we're going to dive with  neptune's army of rubbish cleaners just  on a boat out there we're going to pick  up loads of marine litter  the oceans around us in the uk are  really important because they  host so many types of fish and mammals  and different creatures and so many  different habitats they're very  biodiverse and if you go diving down  there it's amazing  i got in the water i quickly spotted a  lobster pot  a lobster what's the kind of trap that  fishing crews use for catching things  like lobsters  and in this one i can also see a female  spider crab and a few velvet swimming  crabs  by bringing the lobster pot back onto  the boat we can save the lives of these  creatures and reduce marine litter  well done ollie  so i'm going to release this lobster  back into the sea  yeah he's a he's a big one  there you go  when a fisherman has lost their fishing  line or lobster pots due to a storm or  some sharp rocks or something they will  continue to pick up fish lobsters crabs  at least 10 percent of marine litter is  estimated to be made up of fishing waste  abandoned fishing gear is responsible  for harming 66  of marine mammal species it felt really  special when we were going down picking  up these pots taking them out because  there were so many animals inside each  pot and to think that those could have  kept on fishing and all the animals  inside those pots would have just died  and we got them out in the end  and if there's something you'd like to  report on for fyi then the best way is  to join the fyin news club we love it  when you get in touch with us so check  out the details on our website  first.news fyi and you could be on  screen before you know it  check out this incredible photograph  that's just been released showing the  planet jupiter it was taken from the  world's most powerful telescope and you  can even see a giant storm moving across  the planet now that is out of this world  [Music]  now speaking of space how did you get on  with this week's fake news or fact  the story about sean the sheep blasting  off on a moon mission with nasa is a  fact the aardman character is  representing the european space agency  on an uncrewed flight eventually nasa  wants to take the first female astronaut  to the moon  now as for the story about the world's  richest man elon musk buying manchester  united  well he did write that on social media  and lots of people believed him but it  turns out he was only joking so that was  the fake story  [Music]  well that's it for today but we're going  to leave you with some brilliant shots  of animals enjoying a drink during the  hot weather yes sue cross is a farmer in  suffolk but she also loves to take  videos of the animals that pop up on her  farm and just recently they've been  loving the trays of water that she  leaves out for them great work sue and  we'll see you next week bye  [Music]  you

